  "sport": "bandy",
  "title": "Signal skating in a line",
  "description": "The players skate at an easy pace in a line round the ice, a couple of metres apart. The front player has a ball. When the coach whistles or calls, everyone does the task as fast as they can and then skates on: turn and skate backwards, put a hand on the ice, go down on one knee and up again, or a few quick running steps on the skates. The coach mixes the signals so the players have to listen.\n\nNext step: on the signal the back player sprints up the outside of the line to the front. The old leader passes over the ball and the new one leads the line until the next signal. Harder variant: the back player slaloms between the others in the line up to the front – then the gaps must be extra big. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Keep the gaps in the line so nobody skates into the player in front on a signal."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Up from the knee or the ice at once – the first stride comes straight away."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Low position in the turn to backwards, seat down and eyes up."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Sprint with short strides at the start and longer strides once you have speed."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
  "phase": "warmup",
  "difficulty": 1,
